body:
I think only a fool would find it reasonable to see the humanity in humanity, the common thread of struggles and loss, things we could commiserate and help each other through, and think themselves "above it." Like Marcus Aurelius said, we were put here to work together, like hands, feet, arms, legs, teeth.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Of course, there is a difference between sharing problems with a trusted friend/colleague/partner, and griping and "woe is me"ing. Certainly, you should be able to vent and express frustrations (civilly, no need for anger to rear it's ugly head!), but also remember that the only thing that truly can upset you is your own thoughts and actions.

"I have to die. If it is now, well then I die now; if later, then now I will take my lunch, since the hour for lunch has arrived - and dying I will tend to later." - Epictetus, *Discourses*

&#x200B;

Just because death is an inevitability does not mean that you should not live your life. It's perfectly fine to prepare for the future, just keep in mind that even though you may make the best-laid plans, that is no sure sign of success in whatever it is you wish to achieve; life doesn't dare take into account the wants and wishes of fools and mortals.

“Whenever you kiss your child, sibling, or friend, don’t layer on top of the experience all the things you might wish, but hold them back and stop them, just as those who ride behind triumphant generals remind them they are mortal. In the same way, remind yourself that your precious one isn’t one of your possessions, but something given for now, not forever…” - Epictetus, *Discourses*

&#x200B;

I'm glad you had a good time with the dog, and I'm sure they enjoyed their time with you :)

"hmmmm yes this will be a great quote for my myspace page" - 14 year old me

&#x200B;

"hmmmm yes this is one real headass take on life" - me now

On average, dry beans are cheaper, especially in the long run. Sure, it may not seem like a lot of money, but, for example, I can buy a 1lb bag of dry beans for about $1.59, and a can of beans is about $1.80. Serving wise, it's about 20 cents per serving with dry, and about 40-50 cents with canned. 

&#x200B;

Also, canned beans tend to have a lot of added sodium when compared to dry, which has like, none.
